1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:04,418
The Egyptians dragged stones heavier than
a car across the desert,

2
00:00:04,418 --> 00:00:07,101
and for thousands of years nobody knew
how,

3
00:00:07,101 --> 00:00:12,092
until scientists noticed a man in an
ancient painting pouring water on the
sand.

4
00:00:12,542 --> 00:00:15,463
That painting is almost four thousand
years old,

5
00:00:15,463 --> 00:00:20,210
and it shows about 170 men pulling a giant
statue on a sled,

6
00:00:20,210 --> 00:00:25,201
while one man pours water in front of it,
which experts thought was only a ritual.

7
00:00:25,651 --> 00:00:31,918
So in 2014, physicists in Amsterdam pulled
a small sled across a tray of desert sand,

8
00:00:31,918 --> 00:00:34,072
and on dry sand it was hard work,

9
00:00:34,072 --> 00:00:37,923
because the sand kept piling up in front
of it like a wall.

10
00:00:38,373 --> 00:00:42,531
But with just the right amount of water,
the pull dropped to about half,

11
00:00:42,531 --> 00:00:45,129
because tiny drops stick the grains
together,

12
00:00:45,129 --> 00:00:48,594
so the sand gets twice as stiff and the
sled glides over it.

13
00:00:49,044 --> 00:00:53,338
And with too much water, the sand turns to
mud and the sled sinks again,

14
00:00:53,338 --> 00:00:57,035
so the man with the water was doing
engineering, not a ritual.

15
00:00:57,485 --> 00:01:02,026
So even though that painting is seven
hundred years younger than the Great
Pyramid,

16
00:01:02,026 --> 00:01:08,264
the same trick would have let half as many
people pull each of its more than two
million stones across the desert.
